# Captions for carousels 37-72. Slug -> (audience, caption, hashtag_set_key)
NEW_CAPTIONS = {
"37_rest_times": ("B2C", "Nobody times their rest. Everyone thinks they do.\n\nCutting rest on a heavy set doesn't make it harder. It makes it lighter. You just move less weight and call it intensity.\n\nHeavy strength work wants 3-5 minutes. Hypertrophy 90 seconds to 2 minutes. Accessories 60-90. Feeling ready and being recovered aren't the same thing.\n\nHow long are you actually resting? Be honest 👇", "strength"),
"38_cardio_myth": ("B2C", "Cardio doesn't kill your gains. Doing it badly does.\n\nWhat matters: how close it sits to your lifting, how much of it is hard vs easy, and whether you're eating enough to support both.\n\nMost people who lost strength adding cardio also quietly stopped eating enough. That's the actual culprit.", "hybrid"),
"39_soreness": ("B2C", "Soreness is not a scorecard.\n\nIt mostly tells you that you did something unfamiliar: new movement, more range, lots of controlled lowering, or simply far too much on day one.\n\nWeek 4 should feel less sore than week 1. That's the plan working, not failing. Progress lives in the numbers, not the ache.", "general"),
"40_warm_ups": ("B2C", "Your warm-up should look like the session, only lighter.\n\nNot 15 minutes on a treadmill going nowhere. Not static stretching before heavy lifting. If you're squatting, warm up by squatting.\n\nA few minutes raising temperature, then the movement itself loaded progressively. Ten minutes, done.", "general"),
"41_sleep": ("B2C", "Sleep does more for your training than anything you can buy in a tub.\n\nOn short sleep the same weight feels heavier, technique goes first, appetite regulation gets worse, and you recover slower between sessions.\n\nNobody wants this to be the answer. It keeps being the answer 😴", "general"),
"42_training_age": ("B2C", "Your training age matters more than your actual age.\n\nNot how long you've had a membership. Not how long ago you played sport. Consistent, structured months. That's it.\n\nA 45-year-old with two proper years progresses faster than a 22-year-old with none. Which is why the first thing we ask about is experience, not age.", "over40"),
"43_beginner_mistakes": ("B2C", "Five things almost every beginner gets wrong, in order of how much they cost you:\n\nProgramme hopping every fortnight. Chasing soreness instead of progress. Skipping legs then wondering why nothing changes. Adding weight faster than technique holds. Training six days when three would've worked.\n\nNone of these are effort problems. They're all structure problems.", "beginner"),
"44_plateau": ("B2C", "A plateau is usually a recovery problem wearing a training costume.\n\nBefore you change the programme: are you eating enough, are you sleeping, have you added weight for eight straight weeks with no back-off, and are you actually logging or just guessing?\n\nThe answer is rarely a new exercise. It's usually a lighter week.", "strength"),
"45_over_40": ("B2C", "You haven't lost the ability to build muscle. You've lost the recovery you used to take for granted.\n\nYou need longer between hard sessions. Warm-ups stop being optional. Sleep and stress show up in your numbers faster.\n\nWhat hasn't changed: progressive overload still works exactly the same.", "over40"),
"46_women_lifting": ("B2C", "Still saying this in 2026: lifting heavy won't make you bulky.\n\nBuilding visible muscle is difficult, slow and deliberate. It does not happen by accident.\n\nWhat actually happens: you get stronger, you keep muscle while losing fat rather than losing both, bone density improves, and everything outside the gym gets easier.\n\nThe people who look how you want to look are lifting heavy 💪", "strength"),
"47_run_and_lift": ("B2C", "Lifting makes you a faster runner. Doing it wrong makes you a tired one.\n\nHard days hard, easy days genuinely easy. Lift after the run or on a separate day. Never heavy legs the day before a quality session.\n\nMost runners who quit lifting quit because it was scheduled badly, not because it didn't work.", "running"),
"48_consistency": ("B2C", "Three sessions a week for a year beats six a week for a month. It isn't close.\n\nSix a week for a month is 24 sessions. Three a week for a year is 156, and you're still training in December.\n\nBuild the plan around the week you actually have. Two days is a real answer.", "general"),
"49_machines_vs_free": ("B2C", "Machines aren't the easy option. They're a different tool.\n\nGenuinely useful for: training close to failure without a spotter, isolating something lagging, working around a joint that's complaining, and when the gym is heaving and every rack is gone.\n\nFree weights build more than the muscle you're pointing at. Use both.", "strength"),
"50_fasted_training": ("B2C", "Training fasted doesn't burn more fat over a day.\n\nYou burn slightly more during the session and slightly less afterwards. It evens out. That's it.\n\nWhat actually decides fat loss: total energy across the week, protein high enough to hold muscle, and doing it long enough to count.\n\nTrain fasted if you prefer it, just don't expect a bonus.", "general"),
"51_more_isnt_better": ("B2C", "More isn't better. More than you can recover from is worse.\n\nSigns you've overshot: numbers flat or falling for three weeks, dreading sessions you used to enjoy, sleeping badly on training nights, everything aching and nothing specific hurting.\n\nDoing less, properly, isn't a compromise. It's the plan.", "strength"),
"52_perfect_form": ("B2C", "There's no such thing as perfect form. There's form that suits you.\n\nLimb lengths you can't change. Ankle and hip mobility. What you're actually trying to load. Copying someone else's squat because it looks right is how people get hurt.\n\nGood form is repeatable, controlled, and doesn't hurt. If something IS painful, get it looked at properly. That's not a programming problem.", "general"),
"53_genetics": ("B2C", "Genetics decide your ceiling. They don't decide your next four weeks.\n\nAlmost nobody is anywhere near their ceiling. That's the honest bit.\n\nYes, genetics affect how fast you build, where you carry fat and how well you tolerate volume. None of it changes what a well-structured month will do for you.", "general"),
"54_supplements": ("B2C", "Supplements are the last 5%, and you're not there yet.\n\nFix these first: a programme you actually follow, enough protein, enough sleep, enough total food to support the training.\n\nThen, and only then, does anything in a tub make a measurable difference.\n\nWe don't sell supplements and we're not going to recommend any. Speak to a GP or registered dietitian for individual advice.", "general"),
"55_inside_the_app": ("B2C", "What's actually inside your plan.\n\nFive tabs and you only see the ones you need. Train, Morning, Fuel, Recover, Details.\n\nIt opens on your phone at the session you're on. No login, no app store, no download. Save it to your home screen and it's just there.", "product"),
"56_rebuild_feature": ("B2C", "New: build your next block from the one you've already got.\n\nPicked up an injury. Training days changed. Different gym or away for a fortnight. Finished the four weeks.\n\nIt inherits everything you told us first time, no questionnaire twice, and your old plan keeps working, so nothing you logged is lost.", "product"),
"57_reasoning_field": ("B2C", "Tap any exercise and it tells you why it's there.\n\nNot a generic description. Why it's in YOUR plan, given what you told us.\n\nWhich means you know what you're actually training, you can swap it sensibly when the rack's taken, and you stop quietly skipping the ones you don't understand 🧠", "product"),
"58_travel_plans": ("B2C", "Every fitness app assumes you're at home.\n\nTwo weeks away and your plan becomes a document about a gym you can't reach.\n\nPick 'hotel or travelling' and it builds around dumbbells, a bench and a treadmill, with substitutions if it turns out more basic. No pretending you can squat 140 in a basement ✈️", "product"),
"59_goal_calisthenics": ("B2C", "Calisthenics, properly programmed.\n\nBodyweight training and skill progression aren't the same thing. We built the second one.\n\nSkill work first on a fresh nervous system. Progressions gated on prerequisites. You earn the next step. Straight-arm and elbow prep every week, because that's where the injuries actually come from.\n\nNo bar or rings? It says so and builds floor progressions instead.", "calisthenics"),
"60_goal_powerlifting": ("B2C", "Powerlifting, with meet week built in.\n\nPercentages of your estimated max, not fixed weights. Intensity climbs as volume falls. Never heavy squats and heavy deadlifts back to back. Accessories that serve the three lifts rather than chasing set counts.\n\nGot a meet? Tell it how far out and it peaks you into it.", "powerlifting"),
"61_goal_crossfit": ("B2C", "CrossFit, with the safety rails that actually matter.\n\nWhat it won't do: high-rep olympic lifting under heavy fatigue, skill work bolted on after the metcon, heavy barbell and high-volume gymnastics in one session, or kipping volume before strict strength exists.\n\nTechnical failure under load is how people get hurt. So it's programmed out.", "crossfit"),
"62_why_i_built_it": ("Both", "Why I built a training app nobody asked for.\n\nI was paying monthly for a plan an app wrote once, in about four seconds. Three things I couldn't let go of: charging monthly for a one-time generation, plans that never explain themselves, and templates dressed up as personalisation.\n\nSolo founder, UK, no team. Everything here I built, broke and rebuilt.", "buildinpublic"),
"69_qa": ("Both", "Ask me anything.\n\nHow the plans are actually built. Why four weeks not twelve. Building a product on your own. Whatever you're stuck on in your training.\n\nComments open. I answer all of them, and it's actually me on the other end 👋", "buildinpublic"),
"63_twelve_to_four": ("Both", "I built twelve-week plans first. They were worse.\n\nThe longer the plan ran, the vaguer it got. Exercise selection drifted into filler, progression became 'increase as able', and the reasoning went generic. It was guessing about a person it had never met.\n\nCut it to four weeks and the quality jumped immediately. Four weeks is a training block. Twelve is a prediction.", "buildinpublic"),
"64_the_cycle_bug": ("Both", "The bug that quietly deleted people's progress.\n\nPeople repeat a four-week block. Logged weights were keyed by day, not by cycle, so week one of cycle two wrote straight over week one of cycle one. The exact comparison you'd want was gone.\n\nTook me an embarrassingly long time to spot. Every solo founder ships bugs. Most just don't post about them 🙃", "buildinpublic"),
"65_ripped_out_subs": ("Both", "I built a subscription, then deleted it.\n\nEveryone said recurring revenue, so I built recurring revenue. Then I used it and it felt wrong: the work happens once, at the start. Charging monthly for that isn't a service.\n\nSo it came out. One payment, the plan's yours, come back only if you want to. Probably a worse business. Definitely a better product.", "buildinpublic"),
"66_guardrails": ("Both", "Nobody checks the plan before it reaches you. So I had to build the checks.\n\nThat's the uncomfortable bit about generated training plans and most companies won't say it out loud.\n\nHard rules it simply cannot break: no heavy squats and deadlifts back to back, no max attempt in week one, no high-rep olympic lifting under fatigue, no skill progression you haven't earned. Different rules per sport, because people get hurt differently.", "buildinpublic"),
"67_gated_professionals": ("B2B", "I added friction on purpose, and I'd do it again.\n\nCoaches and physios build plans for other people. That's a different responsibility to building one for yourself.\n\nSo the professional route asks for credentials before you can build for anyone, requires you to accept a disclaimer, and needs your approval on every plan before it reaches a client.\n\nFewer signups. Better ones. Clinical judgement never leaves the professional.", "coaches"),
"68_correction_window": ("Both", "What happens when the plan is wrong?\n\nEvery company avoids this question. Here's ours: you've got 48 hours, or until your first logged session, to tell us what's wrong. We fix it. No charge, no argument.\n\nAfter that it's fixed, because by then you're training, not correcting. Small window on purpose. Long enough for a mistake, short enough that one payment isn't an unlimited rewrite.", "buildinpublic"),
"71_deleted_features": ("Both", "Most of building this was deleting things I was proud of.\n\nTwelve-week plans. The subscription. Condition-specific health protocols: too much risk, not enough certainty. A standalone travel product, before it had earned its place.\n\nEvery one took real work and none are in the product. Building is mostly deciding what not to ship.", "buildinpublic"),
"72_ask_for_features": ("Both", "Tell me what to build next. Genuinely. That's how the last three features got prioritised.\n\nAlready on the list because someone asked: training around fixed club sessions, rebuilding a plan when something changes, hotel and travel training, and calisthenics as a proper skill progression.\n\nSmall company, so the person reading your comment is the person building it 👋", "buildinpublic"),
"70_gym_retention": ("B2B", "Most members who quit never had a plan.\n\nThey join in January full of intent, wander between machines for three weeks, see nothing change, stop coming, keep paying, then cancel.\n\nA structured first four weeks is the cheapest retention tool you've got. Branded to your gym, built by your team, on their phone.\n\nRun a gym? DM me. I'd like to hear how you handle onboarding.", "gyms"),
}
